  "conclusion": "The 28-SM treatment is 1.631x slower and reaches 48.68% mean DRAM-read throughput versus 80.33% at 92 SMs. FA2 auto-split remains active: it chooses 7 splits and 168 CTAs at 92 SMs, and 2 splits and 48 CTAs at 28 SMs, keeping roughly 1.8 grid waves per visible SM. The main kernel consumes about 82 KiB shared memory per CTA, limiting residency to one CTA per SM. MPS does not statically partition HBM; the slowdown comes from too few concurrent SM/CTA/warp request generators to saturate the shared HBM bandwidth. Forcing more split waves alone cannot recover 92-SM concurrency.",
